HMG-CoA inhibitor(NON_SIMVASTATIN),PROTEASE INHIBITOR,Moderate Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,Major,Probable,Protease Inhibitors may increase the serum concentration of HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors. Limited data suggest pravastatin may slightly decrease protease inhibitor concentrations.,"This interaction appears to be protease inhibitor-mediated inhibition of the metabolism/elimination of the HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor (likely via CYP3A4 inhibition, though transporter-related or other mechanisms may also be involved), leading to increased HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor concentrations and toxicities.","To minimize the risk of rhabdomyolysis, consider avoiding concurrent use of HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors and protease inhibitors when possible. Start with the lowest possible HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor dose and monitor for signs and symptoms of toxicity (e.g., myalgia, rhabdomyolysis, liver function test abnormalities, etc.) if these agents are used concomitantly. Fluvastatin and pravastatin may be less likely to interact with most protease inhibitors. Lovastatin and simvastatin are generally not recommended for use with protease inhibitors; additionally, with indinavir, use of rosuvastatin is also not recommended. Red yeast rice contains lovastatin (possibly 2.4 mg per 600 mg of red yeast rice) and several other mevinic acid compounds.","Several case reports describe the occurrence of rhabdomyolysis in patients receiving both a protease inhibitor and an HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor.1,2,3 In each of these reports, the patients involved had been receiving either a high-dose HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor and/or multiple drugs capable of interacting with the HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, making it difficult to make inferences regarding the potential risk with any specific protease inhibitor-HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor pair.
