CANNABIS THC,VALPROIC ACID,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,Major,Established,Concurrent use of CANNABIDIOL and VALPROATE may result in increased risk of hepatocellular injury.,additive hepatotoxicity,"Concomitant use of higher doses of cannabidiol and valproate (valproate sodium, valproic acid, or divalproex sodium) increases the risk of transaminase elevations. Consider discontinuation or dose adjustment of cannabidiol and/or valproate if liver enzyme elevations occur (Prod Info EPIDIOLEX? oral solution, 2018).","Concomitant use of cannabidiol and valproate (valproate sodium, valproic acid, or divalproex sodium) increases the risk of transaminase elevations. In clinical trials, resolution of transaminase elevations occurred with discontinuation of cannabidiol or reduction of cannabidiol and/or concomitant valproate in about 66% of cases. In about 33% of the cases, transaminase elevations resolved during continued treatment with cannabidiol, without dose reduction. The incidence of ALT elevations greater than 3 times the ULN was 21% in patients taking concomitant cannabidiol and valproate. Consider discontinuation or dose adjustment of cannabidiol and/or valproate if liver enzyme elevations occur (Prod Info EPIDIOLEX? oral solution, 2018).","Cases of hepatocellular injury up to 18 months after initiation of treatment, particularly in patients taking concomitant valproate, have been reported. In cannabidiol-treated patients, the incidence of ALT elevations greater than 3 times the ULN was 21% in patients taking concomitant valproate (Prod Info EPIDIOLEX? oral solution, 2018).

In a compassionate-use study in 39 adults (mean age, 29.1 years; range, 19 to 62 years) and 42 children (mean age, 10.4 years; range, 2 to 19 years) with treatment-resistant epilepsy, the addition of cannabidiol in the cohort of patients taking background valproate (8 adults and 14 children) was associated with significantly higher ALT and AST levels (although still within normal ranges) compared with patients not taking concomitant valproate. Discontinuation of cannabidiol and valproate in 4 of the children for liver function test elevations greater than 3 times ULN, and discontinuation of valproate only in 1 of the adults for elevations greater than 2 times ULN quickly normalized all cases without any sequelae (Gaston et al, 2017).",MICROMEDEX 2020
