CANNABIS THC,PPI,Moderate Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,Moderate,Established,CYP2C19 Inhibitors (Moderate) may increase the serum concentration of PPI.,"CYP2C19 Inhibitors (Moderate) may increase the serum concentration of Omeprazole,Lanzoprazole. Severity Minor Reliability Rating Excellent","Monitor for increased omeprazole effects and toxicities if combined with moderate CYP2C19 inhibitors. Dose adjustment of omeprazole is not normally required. However, in patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, who may require higher doses, dose adjustment may be considered. Exceptions to this monograph are discussed in separate monographs.","In numerous pharmacokinetic studies with moderate CYP2C19 inhibitors, the omeprazole AUC increased 2.7-fold to 6-fold with concomitant fluvoxamine,1,2,3 3.5-fold to 7-fold with concomitant fluoxetine,4,5 2.1-fold with concomitant cenobamate,6 3-fold with concomitant fedratinib,7 2.1-fold with concomitant moclobemide,8 and 4-fold with concomitant voriconazole.9

The omeprazole prescribing information states that CYP2C19 is primarily responsible for the metabolism of omeprazole and use with CYP2C19 inhibitors will increase omeprazole serum concentrations.10 Dose adjustment of omeprazole is not normally required. However, in patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, who may require higher doses, dose adjustment may be considered.10

The mechanism of this interaction is likely due to inhibition of CYP2C19, an enzyme responsible for omeprazole metabolism.","1. Kamiya C, Inui N, Hakamata A, et al. Effect of co-administered inducer or inhibitor on omeprazole pharmacokinetics based on CYP2C19 genotype. J Pharmacol Sci. 2019;139(4):361-366. [PubMed 30902567]

2. Yasui-Furukori N, Takahata T, Nakagami T, et al. Different inhibitory effect of fluvoxamine on omeprazole metabolism between CYP2C19 genotypes. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2004;57(4):487-494. [PubMed 15025747]

3. Christensen M, Tybring G, Mihara K, et al. Low daily 10-mg and 20-mg doses of fluvoxamine inhibit the metabolism of both caffeine (cytochrome P4501A2) and omeprazole (cytochrome P4502C19). Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2002;71(3):141-152. [PubMed 11907488]

4. Vlase L, Neag M, Popa A, Muntean D, Leucuta SE. Pharmacokinetic interaction between fluoxetine and omeprazole in healthy male volunteers: a prospective pilot study. Curr Ther Res Clin Exp. 2010;71(6):360-368. [PubMed 24688155]

5. Sager JE, Lutz JD, Foti RS, Davis C, Kunze KL, Isoherranen N. Fluoxetine- and norfluoxetine-mediated complex drug-drug interactions: in vitro to in vivo correlation of effects on CYP2D6, CYP2C19, and CYP3A4. Clin Pharmacol Ther. 2014;95(6):653-662. [PubMed 24569517]

6. Xcopri (cenobamate) [prescribing information]. Paramus, NJ: SK Life Science Inc; November 2019.

7. Inrebic (fedratinib) [prescribing information]. Summit, NJ: Celgene Corporation; August 2019.

8. Cho JY, Yu KS, Jang IJ, Yang BH, Shin SG, Yim DS. Omeprazole hydroxylation is inhibited by a single dose of moclobemide in homozygotic EM genotype for CYP2C19. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 2002;53(4):393-397. [PubMed 11966672]

9. Vfend (voriconazole) [prescribing information]. New York, NY: Pfizer; January 2019.

10. Prilosec (omeprazole) [prescribing information]. Zug, Switzerland: Covis Pharma; August 2018.",UPTODATE 2020
CANNABIS THC,WARFARIN,Moderate Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,Moderate,Established,Cannabidiol may increase the serum concentration of Warfarin,Cannabidiol may increase the serum concentration of Warfarin. Severity Moderate Reliability Rating Poor,"Monitor for increased warfarin effects (increased INR, bleeding) if combined with cannabidiol. Warfarin dose adjustments may be required.","A case report describes a 44 year-old man on stable warfarin therapy who started cannabidiol oil therapy (5 mg/kg/day with upward titration) for seizure control.1 The patent's INR increased in a non-linear fashion with increasing doses of cannabidiol. Ultimately, the warfarin dose was decreased by 30% (compared with baseline) during maintenance cannabidiol therapy (35 mg/kg/day).1

The mechanism of this interaction is unknown, but may be due to cannabidiol-mediated inhibition of, or competition for, CYP enzymes involved in warfarin metabolism.1,2","1. Grayson L, Vines B, Nichol K, Szaflarski JP; UAB CBD Program. An interaction between warfarin and cannabidiol, a case report. Epilepsy Behav Case Rep. 2017;9:10-11. [PubMed 29387536]

2. Yamaori S, Koeda K, Kushihara M, Hada Y, Yamamoto I, Watanabe K. Comparison in the in vitro inhibitory effects of major phytocannabinoids and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons contained in marijuana smoke on cytochrome P450 2C9 activity. Drug Metab Pharmacokinet. 2012;27(3):294-300. [PubMed 22166891]",UPTODATE 2020
NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR,HMGCOAinhibitor,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,Major,Established,Concurrent use of RITONAVIR and SIMVASTATIN may result in an increased risk of myopathy or rhabdomyolysis.,inhibition of CYP3A4-mediated simvastatin metabolism,"Coadministration of ritonavir and simvastatin is contraindicated due to the potential for an increased risk of myopathy with possible rhabdomyolysis. If coadministration is required, temporarily suspend simvastatin during the duration of ritonavir treatment (Prod Info ZOCORฎ oral tablets, 2022). Consider using other HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, such as pravastatin or fluvastatin, in combination with ritonavir (Prod Info NORVIRฎ oral capsules, oral solution, 2009; Prod Info KALETRAฎ film coated oral tablets, oral solution, 2009).","Simvastatin is metabolized by CYP3A4 enzymes, and ritonavir is a known potent inhibitor of CYP3A4. Coadministered ritonavir may significantly increase serum concentrations of simvastatin, resulting in simvastatin toxicity (Prod Info NORVIRฎ oral capsules, oral solution, 2009). A case of rhabdomyolysis due to a probable interaction between simvastatin and ritonavir was reported in a 51-year-old female (Cheng et al, 2002). Concomitant use is contraindicated due to the potential for an increased risk of myopathy with possible rhabdomyolysis (Prod Info NORVIRฎ oral capsules, oral solution, 2009; Prod Info KALETRAฎ film coated oral tablets, oral solution, 2009). If coadministration is required, simvastatin therapy should be suspended during the course of ritonavir treatment (Prod Info ZOCORฎ oral tablets, 2022).","A case of rhabdomyolysis due to a probable interaction between simvastatin and ritonavir was reported. A 51-year-old female was admitted to the hospital with upper and lower extremity weakness and body aches for 4 days duration. Her medical history included type 2 diabetes mellitus, HIV infection secondary to a blood transfusion, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and hypercholesterolemia. Her current drug regimen consisted of zidovudine, lamivudine, indinavir, ritonavir, simvastatin, omeprazole, isophane insulin, metformin, meclizine, conjugated estrogens, and medroxyprogesterone. Ritonavir was added one week before admission. On admission, her serum concentrations of creatine kinase (CK), CK-MB isoenzyme, LDH, AST, and ALT were elevated. Urinalysis was positive for hemoglobin and some crystals. The patient received intravenous hydration and sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize her urine. On the third hospital day, muscle and liver enzyme levels peaked. She could not lift her upper or lower extremities. She was able to walk and move her upper extremities by hospital day 6. She was discharged with a serum CK concentration of 8223 international units/liter. The patient's liver and muscle enzymes normalized ten days after discharge. Clinicians should be vigilant in identifying this potentially serious interaction and, if possible, should avoid using statins in patients receiving protease inhibitors (Cheng et al, 2002).",
NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR,COLCHICINE,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Rapid,Major,Established,Concurrent use of COLCHICINE and NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR may result in increased colchicine plasma concentrations and increased risk of colchicine toxicity.,inhibition of CYP3A4- and P-glycoprotein-mediated colchicine metabolism by nirmatrelvir/ritonavir,"Use of colchicine with nirmatrelvir/ritonavir is contraindicated in patients with renal or hepatic impairment due to potential for serious and/or life-threatening reactions (Pfizer Inc, 2021). A colchicine dose adjustment is required in patients with normal renal and hepatic function who are receiving or have received ritonavir within the last 14 days. For the treatment of a gout flare-up with concomitant ritonavir, the adjusted oral dose of colchicine is 0.6 mg for 1 dose, followed by 0.3 mg 1 hour later (repeated no earlier than every 3 days). For prophylaxis of gout, if the original intended oral dose is 0.6 mg twice daily, the adjusted oral dose is 0.3 mg once daily, and if the original intended oral dose is 0.6 mg once daily, the adjusted oral dose is 0.3 mg every other day. In patients with familial Mediterranean fever who are receiving a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor concurrently, the maximum oral daily dose of colchicine is 0.6 mg (may be given as 0.3 mg twice daily) (Prod Info NORVIRฎ oral tablets, oral solution, 2016; Prod Info COLCRYS(TM) oral tablets, 2009).","Use of colchicine with nirmatrelvir/ritonavir is contraindicated in patients with renal or hepatic impairment due to potential for serious and/or life-threatening reactions (Pfizer Inc, 2021). A colchicine dose adjustment is required in patients with normal renal and hepatic function who are receiving or have received ritonavir within the last 14 days. For the treatment of a gout flare-up with concomitant ritonavir, the adjusted oral dose of colchicine is 0.6 mg for 1 dose, followed by 0.3 mg 1 hour later (repeated no earlier than every 3 days). For prophylaxis of gout, if the original intended oral dose is 0.6 mg twice daily, the adjusted oral dose is 0.3 mg once daily, and if the original intended oral dose is 0.6 mg once daily, the adjusted oral dose is 0.3 mg every other day. In patients with familial Mediterranean fever who are receiving ritonavir, the maximum oral daily dose of colchicine is 0.6 mg (may be given as 0.3 mg twice daily) (Prod Info NORVIRฎ oral tablets, oral solution, 2016; Prod Info COLCRYS(TM) oral tablets, 2009).","Concomitant use of a 0.6-mg dose of colchicine and ritonavir 100 mg twice daily for 5 days resulted in 184.4% and 296% increases in colchicine Cmax and AUC, respectively, from baseline in a study of 18 patients (Prod Info COLCRYS(TM) oral tablets, 2009).",
NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR,ALFUZOSIN,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Rapid,Major,Established,Concurrent use of ALFUZOSIN and RITONAVIR may result in an increase in alfuzosin exposure.,inhibition of CYP3A4-mediated metabolism of alfuzosin by ritonavir,"Because there is the potential for increased alfuzosin exposure and hypotension, concomitant use of alfuzosin and ritonavir is contraindicated (Prod Info KALETRAฎ film coated oral tablets, oral solution, 2010).","Although the interaction between alfuzosin, a CYP3A4 substrate, and ritonavir, a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, has not been studied, the interaction between alfuzosin and ketoconazole, another potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, resulted in 2- to 3-fold increases in alfuzosin Cmax and AUC values (Prod Info UROXATRALฎ extended release oral tablets, 2010). Because of the potential for serious reactions, such as hypotension, the concomitant use of these two agents is contraindicated (Prod Info KALETRAฎ film coated oral tablets, oral solution, 2010).","Although the interaction between alfuzosin, a CYP3A4 substrate, and ritonavir, a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, has not been studied, there have been two studies in which repeated doses of ketoconazole, another potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, and alfuzosin resulted in increased alfuzosin Cmax and AUC values. In one study, alfuzosin Cmax and AUC were increased by 2.3- and 3.2-fold, respectively, when patients received repeated ketoconazole doses of 400 mg/day followed by a single 10-mg alfuzosin dose. In another study, alfuzosin Cmax and AUC were increased by 2.1- and 2.5-fold when patients received repeated ketoconazole doses of 200 mg/day followed by a single 10-mg alfuzosin dose (Prod Info UROXATRALฎ extended release oral tablets, 2010).",
NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR,ERGOTAMINE,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Rapid,Major,Established,"Concurrent use of RITONAVIR and ERGOT DERIVATIVES may result in an increased risk of ergotism (nausea, vomiting, vasospastic ischemia).",inhibition of cytochrome P450-mediated ergotamine metabolism by ritonavir,The concurrent use of ergot derivatives and ritonavir is contraindicated.,"The concomitant administration of ritonavir and ergot derivatives is contraindicated due to the potential for serious and/or life-threatening reactions such as acute ergot toxicity characterized by peripheral vasospasm and ischemia of the extremities and other tissues (Prod Info Inviraseฎ, 2003; Pardo Rey et al, 2003; Prod Info Kaletra(TM), 2003).","A 34-year-old HIV positive female presented to the emergency room with dizziness, loss of vision, headache, vomiting, diarrhea, and feeling of cold in her left foot after receiving ergotamine tartrate and ritonavir. Her current drug regimen included ritonavir, lamivudine and stavudine, which she had been taking for the past 3 years to treat HIV infection. She had taken three ergotamine tartrate tablets during the 4 days preceding the onset of signs and symptoms. Physical exam revealed an arterial blood pressure of 160/60 mm Hg, a heart rate of 80 beats/min, the absence of peripheral pulses in all 4 extremities, and acral cyanosis. The patient went in and out of consciousness throughout her stay in the emergency room. She was admitted to the intensive care unit and 24 hours later had a decrease in level of consciousness, as well as arterial hypotension, and orotracheal intubation was necessary. Systemic arteriography demonstrated signs of stenosis and vasospasm in medium and large vessels that diffusely affected the mesenteric, renal, femoral, humeral, and internal carotid arteries, with cerebral hypoperfusion. There was no response to intra-arterial infustion of nimodipine. Treatment with a perfusate containing dobutamine, corticosteroids, and heparin was initiated, but there was no response to treatment. Alprostadil was added to the perfusate for 7 days, but there was no response to treatment. The patient's neurological state deteriorated and she remained in coma vigil. Multiple subcortical infarcts were revealed on a cranial CT scan. Arteriography performed one month after the onset of symptoms revealed vasospasm to have disappeared from all previously involved arteries. Two years after the diagnosis was made there was no change in the patient's neurological state (coma vigil) (Pardo Rey et al, 2003).",
NIRMATRELVIR/RITONAVIR,HYPNOTIC,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Rapid,Major,Established,"Warning:
Concurrent use of RITONAVIR and BZD may result in an increased risk of extreme sedation and respiratory depression.",inhibition of cytochrome P450 3A4-mediated triazolam metabolism by ritonavir,"Concurrent use of ritonavir and BZD is contraindicated (Prod Info Norvirฎ oral capsule, oral solution, 2008).","Concomitant use of ritonavir with BZD is contraindicated as it may increase serum concentrations of BZD, causing a potential risk of extreme sedation and respiratory depression (Prod Info Norvirฎ oral capsule, oral solution, 2008). Clinical trials have shown that ritonavir increases the peak plasma concentration, increases the half-life, reduces the oral clearance, and enhances the clinical effects of BZD (Greenblatt et al, 2000).","A study involving six healthy male volunteers demonstrated that ritonavir administered with triazolam resulted in increased peak plasma triazolam concentration, reduced triazolam clearance to less than 4% of control values (p less than 0.005), prolonged elimination half-life (41 versus 3 hours; p less than 0.005), and enhanced CNS effects of triazolam. Each patient received triazolam 0.125 mg with four scheduled doses of ritonavir 200 mg. These results cannot be extrapolated to higher doses or longer exposures to these two agents (Greenblatt et al, 2000).",
NEFOPAM INJECTION,"OPIOID, TRAMADOL,SSRIs",Moderate Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Rapid,Major,Suspected,"Therapeutic efficacy of Opioid group , Tramadol can be increased when used incombination with Nefopam.",-,Avoid medication interaction,-,www.drugbank.com available 25/5/2024,
IMATINIB,KETOCONAZOLE,Major Severity + Established/Probable Documentation,Delayed,,Established,Concurrent use of IMATINIB and KETOCONAZOLE may result in increased plasma levels of imatinib and/or ketoconazole.,inhibition of CYP3A4-mediated metabolism of imatinib by ketoconazole; inhibition of CYP3A4-mediated metabolism of ketoconazole by imatinib,"Caution is advised when using imatinib, a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor and substrate, together with a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor and substrate such as ketoconazole as coadministration may result in elevated plasma concentrations of either agent (Prod Info NIZORAL? oral tablets, 2014; Prod Info GLEEVEC oral tablets, 2013). If concomitant use is required, monitor patient for signs and symptoms of increased or prolonged pharmacologic effects of ketoconazole (Prod Info NIZORAL? oral tablets, 2014) or imatinib. Dose adjustments may be necessary.","A significant increase in peak plasma concentrations and area under the concentration-time curve (AUC) of imatinib was observed when the drug was coadministered with a single dose of ketoconazole (Prod Info GLEEVEC oral tablets, 2013). However, single dose interaction pharmacokinetics may not accurately reflect toxicity potential for higher therapeutic doses of imatinib given concurrently with a multiple dose regimen of ketoconazole (Dutreix et al, 2004). Caution is advised when using imatinib, a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor and substrate, with a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor and substrate such as ketoconazole as coadministration may result in elevated plasma concentrations of either agent (Prod Info NIZORAL? oral tablets, 2014; Prod Info GLEEVEC oral tablets, 2013). If concomitant use is required, monitor patient for signs and symptoms of increased or prolonged pharmacologic effects of ketoconazole (Prod Info NIZORAL? oral tablets, 2014) or imatinib. Dose adjustments may be necessary.","Dutreix C, Peng B, Mehring G et al: Pharmacokinetic interaction between ketoconazole and imatinib mesylate (Glivec) in healthy subjects. Cancer Chemother Pharmacol 2004; 54:290-294.

Product Information: GLEEVEC oral tablets, imatinib mesylate oral tablets. Novartis Pharmaceuticals Corporation (per FDA), East Hanover, NJ, Oct, 2013.

Product Information: NIZORAL(R) oral tablets, ketoconazole oral tablets. Janssen Pharmaceuticals, Inc (per FDA), Titusville, NJ, Feb, 2014.",
